Work queue (in this order, per Andres - new items get appended at the end unless he
says otherwise). Reordered 2026-08-08 by gain/effort/surface-area/code-practice -
see the note above each moved/merged item for what changed and why.

Reordered again 2026-08-29 into 5 gain/effort/risk tiers (Andres asked for a fresh
pass - the "Smaller polish (low effort, low risk)" section had drifted to also hold
several moderate/high-effort research items, which is part of why this reorg was
worth doing). No item's own text was changed, just where it sits; the
2026-08-29-dated feature-parity block that had been appended as one combined entry
is now split across tiers by what kind of gap each sub-finding actually is (a live
bug vs. a scoped feature gap vs. "needs a decision first vs. a genuine by-design
difference"). Tier order: (1) confirmed bugs - fix before building more on shaky
ground, (2) finish in-progress work / de-risk unknowns before starting anything new,
(3) scoped feature-parity work, (4) cheap low-risk polish, (5) bigger/speculative
work with no near-term pressure. One item stays outside the tiers entirely - it's
blocked on external info, not a priority call.

Same pass also checked whether any OpenCode item had been overtaken by the
serve-API work already shipped - one had: "spawn OpenCode sessions through the
serve API instead of tmux-TUI" was removed outright (create/status/quota/
transcript are all already server/SQLite-backed in code, confirmed 2026-08-29,
not just per stale docs), and the Health checkup item's matching research
sub-clause was trimmed for the same reason (its own UI-split ask stands).

=== Tier 1: confirmed bugs (fix before building more on top) ===

- (2026-08-29, from docs/features.md's per-agent coverage matrix - see
  .claude/feature-atlas/REPORT.md `archived-sessions` finding 1) Archived
  detail/browse uses a Claude-only resolver - the archived *detail* path resolves
  transcripts through `TranscriptService::find_transcript_path` (Claude-specific)
  while paging/attachments already use the agent-agnostic `TranscriptRouter`.
  Antigravity/OpenCode archived rows currently open to "Session not found" - a
  real bug, not just a gap.
- (2026-08-29, from docs/features.md - see .claude/feature-atlas/REPORT.md
  `session-view` finding 1) OpenCode's live detail page can silently stop
  polling - the forward-poll line cursor breaks once any non-renderable row
  precedes the newest renderable message.
- (Andres 2026-08-25) Dashboard polling closes the "last 3 messages" section if
  it was open - the poll should not close a user-expanded card when it
  re-renders that fragment.
- Bug report (Andres 2026-08-24), with a live mobile screenshot: tool-call
  outputs sometimes get attached/"staggered" onto the wrong call. In the
  screenshot (WS2818 LED strip session), a "Bash: pwd && ls -la" entry is
  immediately followed by an "ssh rpi 'hostname; uname -a; cat
  /etc/os-release...'" tool-call entry whose own collapsed result shows
  "/home/user/dev/Rpi/Ws2818 (empty)" - that's the PREVIOUS pwd/ls
  call's output, not the ssh command's - while the ssh command's real
  output (a post-quantum warning + real hostname/uname/os-release text)
  renders as its own separate, un-paired entry below instead of nested
  inside the ssh tool-call entry. Looks like a tool_use/tool_result
  pairing bug, likely in renderEntriesGrouped()/entryIsGroupableToolCall()
  (session.js) or its PHP mirror (TranscriptView) - back-to-back Bash
  calls arriving close together (e.g. during live polling) may be
  mismatching which result attaches to which call. Needs a live repro
  with the actual transcript JSONL lines to see the real call/result
  ordering before diagnosing further.
- Bug report (Andres 2026-08-24), not yet investigated: quota-reset push
  notifications now fire at the right time, but Andres gets TWO for the
  same reset - one correct (reset), one with stale data still claiming
  100% used. Possible race between whatever marks the reset as sent and
  whatever reads the last-known quota state to build the push body -
  check PushDeliveryService::check_and_send_quota_pushes() and
  QuotaService's live-state file read/write ordering for a window where a
  second push can be built/sent off the pre-reset value after the first
  (correct) one already went out.
- Bug report, not yet investigated (Andres 2026-08-23): "maybe some
  entries are missing after reloading a session" - session.php. Unclear
  yet whether this means transcript entries specifically, or something
  else (sidebar Tasks, blocked-prompt state); no repro steps captured
  yet. Needs a proper look - check TranscriptService::read_transcript_page()/
  the initial-load vs poll-time entry set, and whether a hard page reload
  (not a poll) takes a different code path that could drop something a
  poll wouldn't.

=== Tier 2: finish in-progress work / de-risk unknowns before starting anything new ===

- IN PROGRESS (started 2026-08-24, superseding the earlier "long-term, not
  near-term" sequencing below - Andres asked to start directly): support
  Google's Antigravity CLI (`agy`) as a second agent alongside Claude Code,
  via a real AgentAdapter interface. Full research findings, phased plan,
  and an up-to-date "Status at a glance" section (read this first - it's
  kept current, most recently 2026-08-24) live in
  docs/antigravity-adapter-plan.md - read that before touching anything
  here, including a coordination note since Andres runs Claude and his
  own `agy` session against this same repo (not simultaneously, but
  independently). Spawn/identity/hooks/transcript-rendering/quota-polling/
  live model-switching/turn-error-surfacing are done and verified live;
  agent-name + per-agent quota DISPLAY is also done (built by Andres's own
  `agy` session, finished/reviewed/committed by Claude after it hit its
  account quota mid-task). Blocked-prompt detection + answering is done
  for the one prompt shape confirmed live (a tool-permission request) -
  still open: the initial per-folder trust dialog and any AskUserQuestion-
  equivalent Antigravity might have, neither seen live yet. Also still
  open: permission-mode display, and reading this app's own CLAUDE.md-
  style conventions into Antigravity sessions somehow. See also the
  2026-08-29 docs/features.md cross-check (Tier 1/3 items above/below) for
  where the doc's own Antigravity column and this status note now disagree.
- (2026-08-29, from docs/features.md - see .claude/feature-atlas/REPORT.md)
  docs/features.md's whole per-agent matrix predates Codex - it was
  generated 2026-08-26, before Codex was added as a 4th agent
  (`wip: add native Codex integration`, 2026-08-27, and `Integrate Codex
  headless sessions`, 2026-08-29). Every "per agent" row in both the
  coverage matrix and the implementation-status table is missing a Codex
  column entirely - not "Codex is missing this feature" so much as "nobody
  has checked yet." Needs an atlas refresh (`/feature-atlas` or at minimum
  `/feature-atlas-subsystem agent-abstraction`) to actually audit Codex
  against this list rather than guessing from a quick read of freshly-added
  code.
- (2026-08-29, checked in code - branch never deleted, sitting unmerged
  since 2026-08-11) Land `perf/session-list-polling` (still exists locally
  and on origin - NOT deleted, unlike the other stray branches cleaned up
  2026-08-29, since its content is real and genuinely not in master yet).
  Two complementary fixes for the same live-found problem (2026-08-11, with
  several sessions/tabs open, page visibly hanging for a second or two):
  - Server-side: `SessionListCacheStore` (host-agent/lib/Stores/) - a
    very-short-TTL (0.9s, `SESSION_LIST_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS`) file cache
    coalescing concurrent `SessionService::list_all_sessions()` calls (the
    single most expensive thing the host-agent does - a tmux subprocess per
    tracked session plus a full /proc walk) across multiple tabs/pollers
    landing within that window of each other. Doesn't reduce how often any
    ONE poller asks - only prevents redundant concurrent scans from each
    paying for their own.
  - Client-side (session.js): decoupled the sidebar notification dot's
    polling off the main transcript poll cycle (which can be set as low as
    1s) onto its own fixed, much slower interval (15s,
    `startSidebarNotifyPolling()`/`stopSidebarNotifyPolling()`) - the dot
    only needs "glance" freshness, not transcript-poll freshness, and was
    triggering the expensive full scan on every fast tick just to maybe
    flip a dot for sessions the page isn't even showing.
  Needs a real rebase/reconciliation before merging, not a blind merge -
  `SessionService::list_all_sessions()` has changed substantially since
  2026-08-11 (OpenCode/Antigravity/Codex agent support, headless routing,
  all landed after this branch was cut), so the cache-wrapping needs
  re-applying against the current function shape, not assumed compatible.

=== Tier 3: scoped feature-parity work ===

- (2026-08-29, from docs/features.md - see .claude/feature-atlas/REPORT.md
  `agent-abstraction`) Antigravity/OpenCode model & create-option
  reachability - `create_cc_session()` only forwards
  `enable_task_tools`/`starting_mode` to the adapter, so Antigravity's
  `--model`/`--effort` and OpenCode's positional-workdir/`--model`/`--agent`
  options exist in the adapters but aren't reachable through the New
  Session UI.
- (2026-08-29, checked in code against docs/features.md's "Switch mode: ✓"
  for Antigravity - not just a doc read) Antigravity has no LIVE in-session
  mode switch at all, unlike Claude Code - `PromptInteractionService::
  set_mode()` is hardcoded to Claude Code's status-line phrases
  (`PermissionMode::CLAUDE_CODE_MODE_STATUS_PHRASES`) with no Antigravity
  equivalent; Antigravity's `--mode` only ever applies at spawn time (and per
  the item above, isn't even reachable from the New Session UI yet either).
  docs/features.md's flat "✓ / ✓" for Claude Code vs. Antigravity mode
  switching treats these as the same capability when they aren't - worth
  either building a live mode-switch for Antigravity (if one's even possible
  in its CLI) or correcting the doc to show it as spawn-time-only.
- (2026-08-29, from docs/features.md - see .claude/feature-atlas/REPORT.md
  `agent-abstraction`) OpenCode hooks aren't production-wired -
  `check_hooks`/`install_hooks` are honest stubs (only the `csm-permissions`
  plugin ships; the planned `csm-status` plugin doesn't), so OpenCode lacks
  the hook-fed status and session-id self-heal every other agent has.
- (2026-08-29, from docs/features.md - see .claude/feature-atlas/REPORT.md
  `archived-sessions` finding 2) Content search is Claude-Code-only -
  `search_transcripts` and the per-session search box use `TranscriptService`
  (Claude JSONL) exclusively, so Antigravity and OpenCode transcript content
  is invisible to both dashboard search and in-page search.
- (2026-08-29, from docs/features.md) Mode switching is absent for OpenCode
  (no mode vocabulary, only a boolean `--auto`) and effort switching's real
  UI-reachable status is unclear even for Antigravity (see the 2026-08-29
  agy-status cross-check note under the Antigravity item above/Tier 2) - the
  doc itself calls the OpenCode gap "by design, not a bug." Worth an explicit
  decision (extend the vocabulary vs. leave as a genuine per-agent capability
  difference) rather than silently treating it as unfinished parity work.
- (Andres 2026-08-29) Decide on a mitigation for pane-scraping fragility:
  full inventory of every tmux-capture-pane-dependent parsing site (Claude
  Code, Antigravity, OpenCode legacy tmux fallback) plus mitigation options
  now written up in docs/pane-scraping-fragility.md, prompted by a real bug
  the same day (Claude Code's AskUserQuestion tab UI started prefixing its
  question line with "│ ", breaking answer_multi_question()'s exact-string
  pre-flight check - fixed in PromptParser::parse_blocking_prompt()). Pick
  one or more of the doc's mitigation options (loosen exact-match checks to
  structural/substring, freshen+expand the pane fixture library so drift
  gets caught by tests, record a "last verified against CLI version N" note
  per site, or explicitly decide to do nothing beyond fixing bugs as they're
  hit) rather than leaving it as an open list forever.

